= 5
{parent=2008/b}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/b/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/5/a}

Use the convention that insertion returns the insertion tableau $P$ first and the recording tableau $Q$ second. Successive row insertions of $3,4,1,6,7,5,2$ give
$$
\boxed{P=\begin{array}{llll}1&2&5&7\\3&4\\6\end{array},\qquad Q=\begin{array}{llll}1&2&4&5\\3&6\\7\end{array}.}
$$
For example inserting five bumps six to the second row, and the final insertion of two bumps four from the first row and six from the second, creating the third row. The newly created boxes at these steps receive recording labels six and seven respectively.

The deletion input is this pair with the two tableaux interchanged. The symmetry property of <RSK> therefore gives the inverse permutation. One can also reverse-insert the boxes in descending recording-label order. The result is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{del}=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3&4&5&6&7\\3&7&1&2&6&4&5\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Indeed the displayed second row lists the positions of one through seven in the original permutation.

= b
{parent=2008/b/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/5/b}

Let $f^\lambda$ be the number of <standard tableaux> of shape $\lambda$. First the associated <polytabloids> are linearly independent. Order <tabloids> lexicographically by reading each row after sorting its entries. For a column-increasing tableau $t$, the term $\{t\}$ in $e_t$ has coefficient one; every other <tabloid> term has larger row word. To see this, take the first row changed by a nontrivial column permutation. Earlier rows are fixed entry by entry, since each entry belongs to one original column. The changed row can only receive entries from lower positions of those increasing columns, so after sorting it is lexicographically larger. In a linear relation between standard <polytabloids>, choose the smallest row word with a nonzero coefficient. Its own <tabloid> coefficient cannot be cancelled by a larger standard <polytabloid>, a contradiction. Thus $\dim S^\lambda\ge f^\lambda$.

Work first over $\mathbb C$. The <RSK> bijection between permutations and pairs of <standard tableaux> of the same shape gives $\sum_{\lambda\vdash n}(f^\lambda)^2=n!$. The assumed pairwise nonisomorphic irreducible <Specht modules>, and the decomposition of the <regular representation> with irreducible multiplicities equal to their dimensions, give $\sum_\lambda(\dim S^\lambda)^2\le n!$. Combining the two inequalities forces equality term by term: $\dim S^\lambda=f^\lambda$. Hence the independent standard <polytabloids> are a **<basis>**.

The same conclusion holds over every characteristic-zero <field>. All <polytabloid> coordinates in the <tabloid> <basis> are integers, so the ranks of their coordinate <matrices> are the same over any characteristic-zero <field> as over $\mathbb C$. In particular the <dimension> equality and independence transfer without requiring an embedding of the whole <field> into $\mathbb C$.

= c
{parent=2008/b/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/5/c}

The equality $\sum_\lambda(\dim S^\lambda)^2=n!$ exhausts the <dimension> of the <regular representation>. There is no room for another irreducible constituent, since it would contribute its positive squared <dimension>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\{S^\lambda:\lambda\vdash n\}\text{ is a complete irredundant family of irreducible representations in characteristic zero}.}
$$
Each has the standard-<polytabloid> <basis> just proved. The usual split characteristic-zero statement follows over any characteristic-zero <field> because these integral Specht models remain irreducible after extension to an algebraic closure and already exhaust all dimensions there. In the literal wording of the source, <polytabloids> themselves are vectors; it is the <Specht modules> with those bases that form the family of <representations>.

= 6
{parent=2008/b}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/b/6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/6/a}

If the cycle <integer partition>[partition] contains a part $r$, the <Murnaghan–Nakayama rule> is
$$
\boxed{\chi^\lambda((r,\nu))=\sum_{\substack{\mu\subseteq\lambda\\\lambda/\mu\text{ a border strip of size }r}}(-1)^{\operatorname{ht}(\lambda/\mu)}\chi^\mu(\nu).}
$$
A <border strip> is a connected skew <Young diagram> with no $2\times2$ block; its height is its number of occupied rows minus one. The remaining boxes must be a <integer partition>[partition] diagram. The recursion starts with <character> one for the empty diagram at the empty <cycle type>, and an empty sum is zero. Repeated removal for all cycle lengths computes the <character> value.

= b
{parent=2008/b/6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/6/b}

Write the hook cycle <integer partition>[partition] as $\mu=(k,1^{n-k})$, $1\le k\le n$. At the identity, the <representation> <dimension> is $n-1$: a <standard tableau> of shape $(2,1^{n-2})$ is determined by the entry in its top-right box, any one of $2,\ldots,n$.

For $2\le k\le n-2$, the only removable <border strip> of length $k$ is the vertical bottom strip. Its height is $k-1$, and the remaining hook has size $n-k$ and <dimension> $n-k-1$. A strip of length $n-1$ would leave the top-left box but disconnect the removed top-right box from the removed column, so none exists and the value is zero. For $k=n$, the whole hook is a <border strip> of height $n-2$ and contributes $(-1)^{n-2}$. These cases combine as
$$
\boxed{\chi^{(2,1^{n-2})}(k,1^{n-k})=\begin{cases}n-1,&k=1,\\(-1)^{k-1}(n-k-1),&2\le k\le n.\end{cases}}
$$
In particular the formula includes zero at $k=n-1$ and the full-cycle sign at $k=n$.

= c
{parent=2008/b/6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/6/c}

The two <integer partition>[partition] diagrams are transposes, so their characteristic-zero <Specht modules> differ by tensoring with the <sign representation>. A <transposition> has sign minus one. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\chi^{(2,1^{n-2})}((i,j))=-\chi^{(n-1,1)}((i,j))=3-n.}
$$
The middle <character> is $n-3$, since the standard <representation> is the point-permutation <representation> minus its trivial line, and a <transposition> fixes $n-2$ points.

= 7
{parent=2008/b}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/b/7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/7/a}

The <permutation module> $M^\lambda$ has a <basis> consisting of row <tabloids> of shape $\lambda$: tableaux are identified when each row has the same set of entries. A permutation acts by applying it to every entry, and the action is extended linearly over $F$. Its <dimension> is $n!/\prod_i\lambda_i!$.

Here the two singleton rows form an ordered pair of distinct entries, so
$$
\boxed{\dim M^{(n-2,1,1)}=n(n-1).}
$$
A <standard tableau> of the same shape must put one in its top-left corner. Choose two entries from $2,\ldots,n$ for the two remaining boxes of the first column, in increasing order; all other entries then fill the top row in increasing order. Thus the standard-<polytabloid> <basis> gives
$$
\boxed{\dim S^{(n-2,1,1)}=\binom{n-1}2=\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2.}
$$

= b
{parent=2008/b/7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/7/b}

Write $v_{ij}$ for the <tabloid> with second-row entry $i$ and third-row entry $j$. Equivariance gives $\phi(v_{ij})=w_i+w_j$ for every distinct pair. For a tableau $t$, the only nontrivial column antisymmetrizer is on the three entries in its first column. In its alternating sum, pair each column permutation with the permutation obtained by interchanging the two singleton-row positions. Their images under $\phi$ are the same unordered sum of two $w$'s, while their signs are opposite. Hence $\phi(e_t)=0$ for every <polytabloid>, proving $\boxed{S^{(n-2,1,1)}\subseteq\ker\phi}$.

For distinct $i,j,k$,
$$
w_i=\frac12\big[(w_i+w_j)+(w_i+w_k)-(w_j+w_k)\big].
$$
Each summand is an image and two is invertible in <characteristic zero>. Thus every <basis> vector of $M^{(n-1,1)}$ is in the image, proving <surjectivity>. <rank-nullity theorem> then gives
$$
\boxed{\dim\ker\phi=n(n-1)-n=n(n-2).}
$$

= c
{parent=2008/b/7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/7/c}

The stabilizer of an ordered pair is the subgroup $\Sigma_{n-2}$ fixing both entries, so the <permutation module> is $\operatorname{Ind}_{\Sigma_{n-2}}^{\Sigma_n}\mathbf1$. Induce in two stages through $\Sigma_{n-1}$. The first stage is $S^{(n-1)}\oplus S^{(n-2,1)}$. The characteristic-zero <induction branching rule for a symmetric group> adds one box in every possible <integer partition>[partition] position, with multiplicity one. Thus the two second-stage decompositions are
$$
\operatorname{Ind}S^{(n-1)}=S^{(n)}\oplus S^{(n-1,1)},\qquad\operatorname{Ind}S^{(n-2,1)}=S^{(n-1,1)}\oplus S^{(n-2,2)}\oplus S^{(n-2,1,1)}.
$$
By transitivity of induction this yields
$$
\boxed{M^{(n-2,1,1)}\cong S^{(n)}\oplus2S^{(n-1,1)}\oplus S^{(n-2,2)}\oplus S^{(n-2,1,1)}.}
$$
The dimensions are $1$, $2(n-1)$, $n(n-3)/2$ and $(n-1)(n-2)/2$, whose sum is $n(n-1)$, checking the decomposition also at $n=4$.

= 8
{parent=2008/b}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/b/8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/8/a}

Give $M^\lambda$ the invariant <symmetric bilinear form> for which its <tabloid> <basis> is orthonormal. Define
$$
\boxed{D^\lambda=S^\lambda/(S^\lambda\cap(S^\lambda)^\perp).}
$$
In characteristic $p>0$, this quotient is nonzero and simple when $\lambda$ is $p$-regular, meaning that no part occurs $p$ or more times. For other <integer partitions>[partitions] it is zero, so the label “simple <module>” includes the nonzero/$p$-regular qualification.

The <James submodule theorem> states that for every $F\Sigma_n$-<submodule> $U\subseteq M^\lambda$, either $S^\lambda\subseteq U$ or $U\subseteq(S^\lambda)^\perp$. Applied to <submodules> of $S^\lambda$, it says that every proper <submodule> lies in the radical of the restricted form, giving the simplicity of the displayed nonzero quotient.

= b
{parent=2008/b/8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/8/b}

Choose the two <standard tableaux>
$$
t_1=\begin{array}{ll}1&2\\3&4\end{array},\qquad t_2=\begin{array}{ll}1&3\\2&4\end{array},
$$
and use their <polytabloids> $e_1,e_2$, with column-vector coordinates. Writing $[ab\mid cd]$ for the <tabloid> with those two row sets gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
e_1&=[12\mid34]-[23\mid14]-[14\mid23]+[34\mid12],\\
e_2&=[13\mid24]-[23\mid14]-[14\mid23]+[24\mid13].
\end{aligned}
$$
The coefficients at $[12\mid34]$ and $[13\mid24]$ prove independence over every <field>. Applying the generators directly gives
$$
(12)e_1=e_1-e_2,\quad(12)e_2=-e_2,\qquad(23)e_1=e_2,\quad(23)e_2=e_1,
$$
and $(34)$ acts in the same way as $(12)$. Their span is invariant under the generators, contains $e_1$, and contains its whole orbit, hence is the <Specht module>. Thus it is a <basis> without needing a characteristic-zero argument. The <matrices> are
$$
\boxed{\rho(12)=\rho(34)=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-1&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad\rho(23)=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
These integral <matrices> may be reduced in the chosen characteristic.

= c
{parent=2008/b/8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/8/c}

For the <characteristic-three Specht module for the two-by-two partition>, reduce the <matrices> modulo three. The vector $e_1+e_2$ is fixed by every displayed <transposition>: $(12)$ and $(34)$ send it to $e_1-2e_2=e_1+e_2$, and $(23)$ interchanges its two terms. Thus its span is a proper nonzero **trivial <submodule>**.

On the quotient impose $e_2=-e_1$. Each generating <transposition> then acts as minus one, so the quotient is the **<sign representation>**. Consequently
$$
\boxed{0\longrightarrow\mathbf1\longrightarrow S^{(2,2)}\longrightarrow\operatorname{sgn}\longrightarrow0,}
$$
and the two <composition factors> are trivial and sign, each once. In the $D$ notation they are $D^{(4)}$ and $D^{(2,2)}$ respectively. Indeed the restricted <tabloid> form has <Gram matrix> $\begin{pmatrix}4&2\\2&4\end{pmatrix}$, whose characteristic-three radical is precisely the trivial line, so the simple quotient is $D^{(2,2)}$.

The extension does not split: the minus-one <eigenspace> of $\rho(12)$ is the line through $e_2$, but $\rho(23)$ sends that line to the line through $e_1$. Thus there is no sign <submodule> complementing the trivial line, consistent with the failure of semisimplicity.
